Five days is enough if we build this in order. Everything on your sheet rests on four ideas:

1. **Buffers and Henderson-Hasselbalch**: why the ratio of base to acid sets the pH
2. **Buffer calculations**: adding strong acid or base, choosing a buffer, capacity
3. **Titration curves**: each region, the half-equivalence point, the equivalence point
4. **Indicators and polyprotic systems**

We'll start with the first. If you paste the actual sheet, I'll line the rest up to it.

A buffer is a weak acid (HA) and its conjugate base (A⁻) present together. Start from the equilibrium expression, Ka = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA], then take −log of both sides:

**pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])**

Two consequences matter for the exam:
- Only the **ratio** appears, so pH tracks the ratio of base to acid, not their absolute amounts.
- When the ratio is 1, the log term is 0 and pH = pKa. A buffer works well roughly within a ratio of 0.1 to 10, which is pKa ± 1.

Below is 1.00 L of acetic acid/acetate buffer (pKa 4.76). The pH is hidden until you ask for it.

```show_widget
<div style="font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;padding:8px;max-width:520px;color:inherit">
  <div style="font-size:14px;margin-bottom:10px"><b>1.00 L acetic acid / acetate buffer</b> (pKa 4.76)</div>
  <div style="font-size:13px;margin-bottom:4px">HA (acetic acid): <span id="haL">100</span> mmol</div>
  <div style="background:rgba(128,128,128,.2);border-radius:4px;height:20px;margin-bottom:10px"><div id="haB" style="height:100%;border-radius:4px;background:#d85a30;width:50%;transition:width .3s"></div></div>
  <div style="font-size:13px;margin-bottom:4px">A⁻ (acetate): <span id="aL">100</span> mmol</div>
  <div style="background:rgba(128,128,128,.2);border-radius:4px;height:20px;margin-bottom:14px"><div id="aB" style="height:100%;border-radius:4px;background:#378add;width:50%;transition:width .3s"></div></div>
  <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:12px">
    <button onclick="addAcid()" style="padding:6px 10px;cursor:pointer">Add 10 mmol HCl</button>
    <button onclick="addBase()" style="padding:6px 10px;cursor:pointer">Add 10 mmol NaOH</button>
    <button onclick="resetAll()" style="padding:6px 10px;cursor:pointer">Reset</button>
  </div>
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;font-size:14px">
    <button onclick="togglePH()" style="padding:6px 10px;cursor:pointer">Show / hide pH</button>
    <span>pH: <b id="ph">?</b></span>
  </div>
  <div id="msg" style="font-size:12px;margin-top:8px;min-height:16px"></div>
</div>
<script>
var ha=100,a=100,show=false,pKa=4.76;
function render(){
  document.getElementById('haL').textContent=ha;
  document.getElementById('aL').textContent=a;
  document.getElementById('haB').style.width=(ha/200*100)+'%';
  document.getElementById('aB').style.width=(a/200*100)+'%';
  var msg=document.getElementById('msg');
  if(ha<=0||a<=0){msg.textContent='One component is used up: this is no longer a buffer.';document.getElementById('ph').textContent=show?'(not a buffer)':'?';return;}
  msg.textContent='';
  var p=pKa+Math.log10(a/ha);
  document.getElementById('ph').textContent=show?p.toFixed(2):'?';
}
function addAcid(){if(a>=10){a-=10;ha+=10;}else{a=0;}render();}
function addBase(){if(ha>=10){ha-=10;a+=10;}else{ha=0;}render();}
function resetAll(){ha=100;a=100;render();}
function togglePH(){show=!show;render();}
render();
</script>
```

**Before you click anything:** if you add 0.010 mol of HCl to this buffer, which bar shrinks and which grows, and roughly how far do you think the pH moves? Then use the buttons to check your prediction.
